WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:05.540
- In a few moments, the room will go dark,

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.480
the screen will light up,

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:11.200
and we will watch just a
few of the brilliant moments

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.873
of the career of a great actor,

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:19.660
a great star who makes
all the rest of us actors

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:21.370
proud to be actors.

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:23.521
This is Liv Ullmann.

00:00:23.521 --> 00:00:26.521
(audience applauds)

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.970
Most of us would be happy to sit back

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:35.520
and watch nothing but her film
clips for the next two hours,

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.520
but we have other matters to attend to.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:43.520
So let me offer up the
much shorter Lithgow list

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:49.230
of reasons why Liv so abundantly
deserves tonight's tribute.

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:53.160
First there is Liv's legendary history

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.631
as the collaborator and muse
of the iconic Ingmar Bergman.

00:00:57.631 --> 00:01:01.220
(audience applauds)

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.700
She appeared in 10 Bergman films,

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.547
among them "Persona,"
"Cries and Whispers,"

00:01:06.547 --> 00:01:10.180
"Face to Face," and "Autumn Sonata."

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:12.960
For those few who claim she
would never have been called

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:16.150
one of our greatest actors
without Ingmar Bergman,

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:20.070
I would answer Bergman would
probably never have been called

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.884
one of our greatest filmmakers
without Liv Ullmann.

00:01:22.884 --> 00:01:25.884
(audience applauds)

00:01:31.295 --> 00:01:36.295
After a long career creating
a film legacy second to none,

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:41.590
Liv turned her attention to directing.

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:46.400
She embraced this new role
with a joy and passion,

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.590
an early pioneer in the struggle

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:53.500
to right the imbalance
between men and women in film,

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:55.850
guiding and mentoring the likes

00:01:55.850 --> 00:01:59.240
of Jessica Chastain in "Miss Julie,"

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:03.380
and Cate Blanchett in her
definitive stage performance

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:07.327
as Blanche DuBois in "A
Streetcar Named Desire."

00:02:08.337 --> 00:02:09.170
(audience applauds)

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:11.540
For those of you from Beverly Hills,

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:14.303
a streetcar is a
multi-passenger vehicle on rails

00:02:14.303 --> 00:02:15.560
(audience laughs)

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.810
typically operated on city streets.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:22.480
Now the great power of Liv's
achievements as an actor

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.293
and as a director arises

00:02:25.293 --> 00:02:30.293
from her boundless capacity for empathy.

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:32.480
But yet another demonstration

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:34.750
of that empathy is her advocacy

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:39.750
dating back to the 1970s
on behalf of refugees.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:45.247
Tirelessly working with
such organizations as UNICEF

00:02:45.247 --> 00:02:47.630
and the International Rescue Committee,

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:50.610
Liv has heightened awareness of the plight

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:53.200
of refugees all over the world,

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.130
and raised millions of
dollars for their relief.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:00.783
Her commitment to the refugee
issue shines especially bright

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:05.433
at this deeply troubling
historical moment.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.040
In this cause, she has
never ceased to follow

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:13.753
the concise rule she
has forged for herself:

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.733
Don't turn away.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:20.053
Don't turn away.

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:22.830
It's a phrase that could be used

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.740
with equal accuracy to describe

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.290
the profound artistry of Liv Ullmann.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.800
Tonight is your moment of course, Liv,

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:38.580
but let me share my own
memory of her special magic.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:42.170
Years ago, I acted with her on Broadway

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:44.827
in Eugene O'Neill's "Anna Christie."

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:48.600
Liv played the title character of Anna,

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.820
a sad prostitute hungry for redemption.

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:54.360
The show was directed

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:59.353
by the brilliant, volcanic Jose Quintero.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:03.640
One day halfway through rehearsals,

00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:06.870
I watched Jose take Liv

00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:10.393
into the heart of darkness,

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.750
invoking for her the desperation

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:19.130
and degradation of a life of prostitution

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:22.130
by calling on his own childhood memories

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:25.343
of dockside prostitutes in Panama City.

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:29.390
I was not in the scene they
were rehearsing that day.

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:32.863
I was just sitting and watching them work,

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:37.293
but under Jose's spell,

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:44.017
I saw Liv fearlessly absorb, internalize,

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:50.910
and bring to life the pain
and loss of a broken woman

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:53.823
far outside her own experience.

00:04:54.970 --> 00:04:59.440
It was a moment of aching empathy

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:00.980
which she managed to sustain

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:03.880
for the next six months of performance.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:07.170
I've seen a lot of great acting in my day,

00:05:07.170 --> 00:05:09.393
but I've never seen anything like that.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.310
But don't take my word for it.

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:18.110
Take a moment to watch this
remarkable woman at work.

00:05:18.110 --> 00:05:19.653
I promise you one thing:

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:22.113
You won't turn away.

